# Per-path token ceilings, set from COMMIT ONE (V9).
#
# This is the lesson nanokit exists to carry, transplanted as a number:
# itok set its first ceiling at 19,542 -- the size its SPEC.md ALREADY was
# -- and then raised it four times, to 27,500. A ceiling that arrives after
# the growth RATIFIES it; one that arrives first CATCHES it.
#
# RAISED TWICE IN ONE DAY. 4500 -> 5100 when T4 landed and the ceiling fired
# again at 4,587. Same procedure as below, in the same order: cut duplication
# first (V13 and §I each restated B3's record; -58), re-measure (4,529), then
# size by V21's rule -- 5,100 is ~12.6% over.
#
# The note below predicted this and said it would be worth knowing. It is:
# T4 cost +484 tokens MEASURED, and T5, T7, T8 remain, so on this trajectory
# 5,100 buys about one more task. That is the deliberate choice -- a ceiling
# sized to the trajectory (6,000 was the alternative, and was rejected) would
# stop catching anything in the meantime, which is what a ceiling is for.
# Expect a third raise, and expect it to be reviewed the same way.
#
# What did NOT happen, and is the point: no rejected-option record was traded
# for bytes (V16), and no threshold moved before the duplication pass.
#
# RAISED ONCE, 4k -> 4500, on the day M2 landed. The ceiling FIRED at 4,105
# against 4,000 -- which is the whole point of setting it at 2x the size the
# file then was, rather than at the size it already had.
#
# The review it triggered, in order, because the order is the discipline:
#
#   1. Look for duplication first. Found some: T6 and V24 restated B1's and
#      B2's records, written before those rows existed. Cut, -60 tokens.
#   2. Re-measure. 4,045 -- still over. What remained was prose, not
#      restatement, and a spec compacted to satisfy a number trains the
#      same reflex as a ceiling raised to satisfy one.
#   3. Only then size the new number BY RULE, not by what fits: V21 says a
#      ceiling wants ~12% headroom, and 4,500 is 11% over 4,045.
#
# The growth was a MILESTONE, not creep: V24, B1, B2 and a rewritten T6,
# each carrying a runner or evidence, and V16 forbids trading a
# rejected-option record for bytes. Four tasks remain, so this will be
# tested again -- and a second raise inside a task or two would be evidence
# that 4,500 was also chosen too small, which is worth knowing.
#
# Checked by `itok check` (`--bpe`, so the verdict is deterministic). T4 has
# landed and did NOT take this over: `nanokit check` reads SPEC.md's structure,
# not its size. T5's `derive` reports sizes; whether the GATE moves here is a
# decision for T5, not an assumption to leave standing in a comment.
SPEC.md    5100
